window.docContents[162].resolve({"/Distance/#section-moore-graphs":{"contents":"A graph attaining the Moore bound is called a Moore graph. Equality forces every\nlayer to have the largest possible size, which (one can show) makes a Moore graph of\ndiameter k with d ≥ 1 a (d + 1)-regular graph of girth 2 * k + 1, so that it\nattains the cage bound as well. (For d = 0 the single edge K[2] attains the Moore\nbound but has no cycles.)\n\nThe simplest examples are the complete graphs: for d ≥ 1, K[d + 2] is\n(d + 1)-regular, has diameter 1 and girth 3, and both bounds with k = 1\nevaluate to d + 2. Next come the odd cycles: for k ≥ 1, C[2 * k + 1] is\n2-regular (so d = 1), has diameter k (ediam_cycleGraph) and girth 2 * k + 1\n(egirth_cycleGraph), and both bounds evaluate to 2 * k + 1, as the following\ncomputation checks.\n\nexample (k : ℕ) : 1 + (1 + 1) * ∑ i ∈ Finset.range k, 1 ^ i = 2 * k + 1 := by\n  simp [add_comm]\n\n\nThe most famous Moore graph is the Petersen graph petersenGraph = Kn[5, 2]. Recall\nthat this is the graph with vertices given by subsets of Fin 5 of size 2, with\ntwo subsets adjacent iff they are disjoint.\n\nIt has 10 vertices, is 3-regular, and has diameter 2 and girth 5, so it\nattains both bounds with d = 2 and k = 2: 10 = 1 + 3 * (1 + 2).\n\nThe Petersen graph by distance from the root r = \\{0, 1\\}\n\nlemma card_petersenGraph : Fintype.card {s : Finset (Fin 5) // s.card = 2} = 10 := by\n  decide\n\n\nlemma IsRegularOfDegree.petersenGraph : petersenGraph.IsRegularOfDegree 3 := by\n  intro v\n  decide +revert\n\n\nWe cannot ask decide for the distances directly, since it cannot evaluate edist\n(Distance Layers). Instead we reduce everything to facts about\nadjacency, which decide can easily check.\n\nTwo distinct non-adjacent vertices are 2-subsets sharing exactly one element, so\ntheir union has three elements, and the remaining two elements of Fin 5 form a\ncommon neighbour.\n\nlemma petersenGraph_exists_adj_adj : ∀ u v, u ≠ v → ¬ petersenGraph.Adj u v →\n    ∃ w, petersenGraph.Adj u w ∧ petersenGraph.Adj w v := by\n  decide\n\n\nNo three 2-subsets of Fin 5 are pairwise disjoint, so there are no triangles.\n\nlemma cliqueFree_petersenGraph_three : petersenGraph.CliqueFree 3 := fun t ht => by\n  obtain ⟨a, b, c, hab, hac, hbc, rfl⟩ := is3Clique_iff.mp ht\n  revert a b c\n  decide\n\n\nTwo distinct vertices have at most one common neighbour, which must be disjoint\nfrom their union, a set of at least three elements. By\ncycleGraph_four_free_iff_common_neighbor_unique, this rules out a copy of C[4].\n\nlemma cycleGraph_four_free_petersenGraph : C[4].Free petersenGraph :=\n  cycleGraph_four_free_iff_common_neighbor_unique.mpr (by decide)\n\n\nThe diameter is at most 2 by petersenGraph_exists_adj_adj. It is at least 2,\nsince by the Moore bound a 3-regular graph of diameter at most 1 has at most\n1 + 3 = 4 vertices.\n\nlemma ediam_petersenGraph : petersenGraph.ediam = 2 := by\n  refine le_antisymm (ediam_le_of_edist_le fun u v => ?_) ?_\n  · by_cases huv : u = v\n    · simp [huv]\n    by_cases hadj : petersenGraph.Adj u v\n    · exact (edist_eq_one_iff_adj.mpr hadj).le.trans (by norm_num)\n    obtain ⟨w, huw, hwv⟩ := petersenGraph_exists_adj_adj u v huv hadj\n    exact (edist_le (cons huw (cons hwv nil))).trans (by simp)\n  · by_contra! h\n    have := moore_bound petersenGraph 2 1\n      (fun v => (IsRegularOfDegree.petersenGraph v).le)\n      (Order.le_of_lt_add_one (by simpa [one_add_one_eq_two] using h))\n    rw [Nat.card_eq_fintype_card, card_petersenGraph] at this\n    norm_num at this\n\n\nThe girth is at least 5 by five_le_egirth_of_cliqueFree_three_of_free. For the\nupper bound, as for the complete bipartite graphs, we exhibit a copy of a cycle\ngraph: the pentagon {0, 1} — {2, 3} — {0, 4} — {1, 2} — {3, 4} — {0, 1} is a copy\nof C[5], and decide checks that the map is an injective homomorphism.\n\nlemma egirth_petersenGraph : petersenGraph.egirth = 5 := by\n  refine le_antisymm ?_ (five_le_egirth_of_cliqueFree_three_of_free\n    cliqueFree_petersenGraph_three cycleGraph_four_free_petersenGraph)\n  have h5 : C[5] ⊑ petersenGraph :=\n    ⟨⟨⟨![⟨{0, 1}, rfl⟩, ⟨{2, 3}, rfl⟩, ⟨{0, 4}, rfl⟩, ⟨{1, 2}, rfl⟩,\n      ⟨{3, 4}, rfl⟩], by decide⟩, by decide⟩⟩\n  exact h5.egirth_le.trans ((egirth_cycleGraph 2).trans_le (by norm_num))\n\n\nMoore graphs are rare. Besides C[5] and the Petersen graph, the only known Moore\ngraph of diameter 2 is the Hoffman–Singleton graph, which is 7-regular with 50\nvertices. The Hoffman–Singleton theorem A. J. Hoffman and R. R. Singleton, On Moore graphs with diameters 2 and 3, IBM J. Res. Develop. 4 (1960), no. 5, 497–504.\n(Strongly Regular Graphs) shows that any other would be\n57-regular, and whether one exists is a famous open problem. For diameter at least\n3, it is known that the only Moore graphs are the odd cycles.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Distance\u0009Moore and Cage Bounds","header":"11.4.7. Moore Graphs","id":"/Distance/#section-moore-graphs"},"/Maps/#section-vertex-transitivity-of-hypercubes":{"contents":"The hypercube is vertex-transitive: \"translation\" by a fixed vertex t, i.e.\ncomponentwise xor with t, is an automorphism of Q[n], and translating by\nu.xor v sends u to v.\n\nComponentwise xor (symmetric difference) of two hypercube vertices./-- Componentwise xor (symmetric difference) of two hypercube vertices. -/\ndef HypercubeVert.xor : {n : ℕ} → HypercubeVert n → HypercubeVert n → HypercubeVert n\n  | 0,     _,      _      => ()\n  | _ + 1, (v, a), (w, b) => (v.xor w, a ^^ b)\n\n\nNotation v ⊕ₕ w for the componentwise xor of hypercube vertices\nv w : HypercubeVert n, i.e. addition in (ℤ/2)ⁿ. (We use ⊕ₕ rather than ⊕ to\navoid clashing with the Sum notation α ⊕ β.)\n\n@[inherit_doc HypercubeVert.xor]\nscoped infixl:65 \" ⊕ₕ \" => HypercubeVert.xor\n\n\nxor-ing twice by the same vertex is the identity, so translation is an\ninvolution./-- xor-ing twice by the same vertex is the identity, so translation is an\ninvolution. -/\n@[simp]\nlemma HypercubeVert.xor_xor {n : ℕ} (v t : HypercubeVert n) : (v ⊕ₕ t) ⊕ₕ t = v := by\n  induction n with\n  | zero => rfl\n  | succ n ih =>\n    obtain ⟨v, a⟩ := v; obtain ⟨t, s⟩ := t\n    exact Prod.ext (ih v t) (by cases a <;> cases s <;> rfl)\n\n\nWe record this using Mathlib's Function.Involutive, which gives us injectivity\nand the inverse equivalence for free.\n\nlemma HypercubeVert.xor_involutive {n : ℕ} (t : HypercubeVert n) :\n    Function.Involutive (· ⊕ₕ t) :=\n  (HypercubeVert.xor_xor · t)\n\n\nxor-ing u into u ⊕ₕ v recovers v./-- xor-ing u into u ⊕ₕ v recovers v. -/\n@[simp]\nlemma HypercubeVert.xor_xor_cancel {n : ℕ} (u v : HypercubeVert n) :\n    u ⊕ₕ (u ⊕ₕ v) = v := by\n  induction n with\n  | zero => rfl\n  | succ n ih =>\n    obtain ⟨u, a⟩ := u\n    obtain ⟨v, b⟩ := v\n    exact Prod.ext (ih u v) (by cases a <;> cases b <;> rfl)\n\n\nTranslation by t is injective./-- Translation by t is injective. -/\n@[simp]\nlemma HypercubeVert.xor_left_inj {n : ℕ} {v w t : HypercubeVert n} :\n    v ⊕ₕ t = w ⊕ₕ t ↔ v = w :=\n  (HypercubeVert.xor_involutive t).injective.eq_iff\n\n\nTranslating both vertices of a potential edge by t preserves adjacency in\nQ[n]./-- Translating both vertices of a potential edge by t preserves adjacency in\nQ[n]. -/\nlemma HypercubeVert.xor_adj {n : ℕ} (v w t : HypercubeVert n) :\n    Q[n].Adj (v ⊕ₕ t) (w ⊕ₕ t) ↔ Q[n].Adj v w := by\n  induction n with\n  | zero => simp\n  | succ n ih =>\n    cases v; cases w; cases t\n    simp [ih, HypercubeVert.xor]\n\n\nTranslation of Q[n] by a fixed vertex t, via componentwise xor, as a graph\nautomorphism./-- Translation of Q[n] by a fixed vertex t, via componentwise xor, as a graph\nautomorphism. -/\ndef hypercube.translate {n : ℕ} (t : HypercubeVert n) : Q[n] ≃g Q[n] where\n  toEquiv := (HypercubeVert.xor_involutive t).toPerm\n  map_rel_iff' := HypercubeVert.xor_adj _ _ t\n\n\nThe hypercube Q[n] is vertex-transitive./-- The hypercube Q[n] is vertex-transitive. -/\nlemma isVertexTransitive_hypercube (n : ℕ) : Q[n].IsVertexTransitive :=\n  fun u v => ⟨hypercube.translate (u ⊕ₕ v), HypercubeVert.xor_xor_cancel u v⟩\n\n\nWe do not describe the full automorphism group of Q[n] here: it is the wreath\nproduct ℤ/2 ≀ Sₙ, generated by the translations above together with permutations\nof the coordinates. While our construction of the hypercube using the vertices\nHypercubeVert n is very convenient for inductive proofs, to talk about the\n\"coordinates\" of a vertex it is better to use Fin n → Bool. We return to this in\nThe Hypercube and the Hamming Cube, where we give a graph\nisomorphism Q[n] ≃g hypercubeHamming n between our hypercube and the Hamming cube\non Fin n → Bool, and then use it to identify the automorphism group of Q[n] as\nℤ/2 ≀ Sₙ.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Maps\u0009Automorphisms","header":"5.6.2. Vertex-Transitivity of Hypercubes","id":"/Maps/#section-vertex-transitivity-of-hypercubes"}});